WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:12.554
Migrating a WordPress site to PageMotor. This is the honest end-to-end walkthrough: what carries over cleanly, what needs rebuilding, and the surprises to plan for.

2
00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:17.386
Two to four hours in total, and most of that is transferring images.

3
00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:26.925
First, check it is the right guide for your site. It covers Thesis and Focus WordPress sites moving into an existing PageMotor installation.

4
00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:38.984
It will not work for Divi, for Gutenberg block-based sites, or for WooCommerce shops: those have different export shapes and different importers, and the guide points them at the right tools.

5
00:00:39.684 --> 00:00:48.080
Get three downloads before you begin. The EP WP Exporter, a WordPress plugin that exports your content and theme data.

6
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:55.123
The PageMotor Migrator, a command-line translator that converts the export into PageMotor's format.

7
00:00:55.123 --> 00:01:03.306
And the Focus Bridge Theme, which bridges your Focus CSS classes so your existing styles work without modification.

8
00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:09.638
You will also want SFTP access, and PHP 7 or newer on the machine running the translator.

9
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:16.340
Now three surprises that catch nearly everyone. The first is that the images are on you.

10
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:24.662
The translator rewrites every image path to point at the new location in PageMotor, but it does not move the actual files.

11
00:01:24.662 --> 00:01:34.347
You will need your SFTP credentials and ten to thirty minutes. Most migrations feel roughly on track until this step, and then slow down here.

12
00:01:35.047 --> 00:01:42.655
The second surprise: after importing your theme data the site has no CSS and looks completely broken.

13
00:01:42.655 --> 00:01:49.962
That is normal and expected, but only if you know about it in advance. The fix is a single click.

14
00:01:49.962 --> 00:01:59.228
Open Admin, Theme Settings, Design Options, and click Save. That triggers a full CSS recompile from your imported settings.

15
00:01:59.228 --> 00:02:03.747
Do not skip it, and do not be alarmed when you first see it.

16
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:14.049
The third surprise: payment forms look right but do not work. Plain contact forms migrate cleanly and work fine once EP Email is installed.

17
00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:23.651
But anything with a payment back-end, PDF generation, file upload or conditional logic will appear in the right place and render correctly,

18
00:02:23.651 --> 00:02:34.772
while the processing that made it charge a card does not come with it. If your site has order forms, read that troubleshooting entry before you start, not after.

19
00:02:35.472 --> 00:02:47.217
The good news is how much migrates on its own. Pages at every level of nesting, with the hierarchy preserved. Posts under a paginated blog with thumbnails.

20
00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:55.930
Top-level menus and footer widgets. Your Focus skin CSS, custom CSS and design options, so the site looks the same.

21
00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:04.265
Featured images, YouTube embeds converted to lazy loading, and internal links rewritten to hierarchical paths.

22
00:03:04.965 --> 00:03:14.470
And here is what you rebuild by hand. Dropdown and multi-level menus. Dynamic sidebar widgets, so archives, categories and tag clouds.

23
00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.932
Extra user accounts, though author attributions are preserved in the content.

24
00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:32.133
Gutenberg block content, WooCommerce products and orders, and newsletter lists all need a separate path, and multilingual content has to be migrated one language at a time.

25
00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:38.621
The job runs in four stages. Export from WordPress. Translate the export.

26
00:03:38.621 --> 00:03:50.991
Import into PageMotor, backing up the database first, installing the bridge theme, recompiling the CSS and transferring the images. Then configure and test.

27
00:03:51.691 --> 00:04:00.754
The guide has every step in order, the page map to review before importing, the payment-form troubleshooting to read before you start,

28
00:04:00.754 --> 00:04:04.879
and where to go if you are on Divi, Gutenberg or WooCommerce.

